id: pm-rzojed
title: Secret guard misses a credential inside a POSIX recovery command whose argument the renderer single-quoted with apostrophe splices
description: "quotePosixCommandArg single-quotes an argument that holds !, CR or LF, or ends in a backslash (pm-oq893h, pm-jzrtsr), and spells each apostrophe in it as the splice '\\''. When such an argument holds a key-named assignment whose value is itself quoted (a note with a line break and a quoted password-style assignment), the detector judges the rendered command, sees the key inside the first single-quoted piece and the value starting at a closing quote, and reports nothing; it reports the same argument raw and in the double-quoted spelling main rendered before line breaks were single-quoted. The gap existed for !-holding arguments since pm-oq893h and widened with pm-jzrtsr. Found by the Codex round 4 review of PR #1789."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[4]: "area:security",recovery,secret-guard,shell-quoting
created_at: "2026-11-26T17:15:46.601Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-26T17:16:05.155Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: "The detector reads a POSIX single-quoted word with '\\'' splices as the shell word it spells before judging a key-named assignment inside it; A rendered recovery command, raw and inside a JSON string, carries the same findings as its unrendered argument for every spelling quotePosixCommandArg emits; A differential against the published bundle shows no other verdict change"
parent: pm-qwoy
risk: medium
confidence: medium
expected_result: A credential in an argument is reported in the rendered recovery command whatever quoting the renderer chose
actual_result: An argument with a line break or ! and a quoted credential assignment is reported raw but not in its single-quoted rendering
